WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
Plan the model card from the start.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:09.069
You will publish a model card with your adapter, so collect its contents as you go instead of reconstructing them at the end.

3
00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:25.285
Hugging Face’s model card guidance asks for a description of the model, intended uses and limitations (including biases), training parameters, training data, and evaluation results, and its metadata supports fields for the license and for base_model,

4
00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:29.341
which links an adapter or fine-tune to the model it was built on.

5
00:00:29.621 --> 00:00:40.587
The idea comes from Mitchell and colleagues (2019), who proposed model cards so that anyone using a model could see how it was evaluated and where it shouldn’t be used.

6
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:57.229
For this project the card should state: the base model and its license, with any attribution it requires; what the adapter does and the input format it expects; the training data in outline (sources, size, how it was labeled or generated, and what was excluded);

7
00:00:57.509 --> 00:01:08.773
the benchmark results with intervals, against the base and frontier models, with the test-set description; known failure modes from your failure analysis; and uses it is not suitable for.

8
00:01:09.053 --> 00:01:13.085
If your training data can’t be published, say so and describe it instead.

9
00:01:13.365 --> 00:01:24.501
If you can’t publish the adapter at all because of the base license or your data, write the card anyway and keep the adapter private; the requirement is the card’s honesty, not the upload.
